  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through Sunday)\nIssued at 145 AM MDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\nBy Wednesday, an impressive H5 low will slide south into NorCal \nwhile the upper level ridge begins expanding westward across the \nlower MS River Valley. This will tighten up the monsoon plume and \ndirect deep south to north steering flow over NM. After two days of \nheavy rainfall in some areas, the risk for flash flooding will be \nexacerbated by widespread showers and storms with rainfall rates \nexceeding >2 \"/hr. The 00Z RRFS shows storms latching on to north/\nsouth oriented terrain and dumping rainfall amounts >2.5\". Even \nthe median NBM 12-hr QPF has amounts >1.5\" over broad areas with \n90th %tile amounts above 2\". WPC has added a large 'Moderate Risk'\narea to parts of northern and central NM so the potential for a \nFlood Watch is rising for parts of the region, especially Ruidoso.\n\nThe rich moisture plume persists Thursday with more south/north \nsteering flow and a 'Slight Risk' of excessive rainfall over the \nentire area. Widespread cloud cover from Wednesday night may limit \nthe amount of instability available so confidence does decrease by \nthis time. Any showers and storms that do form will still be very \nefficient rainmakers. The upper level low over CA begins to open up \nand lift east across the Great Basin Friday. This may assist forcing \nfor more showers and storms over the area with additional heavy \nrainfall possible. Widespread total QPF of 1 to 2\" is likely by \nFriday night with localized amounts >3\" possible. Even the 00Z LREF \nprobability of >2\" is already above 40% for most high terrain areas \nwith a 20-30% chance of >3\" for the San Juan/Tusas Mts.",
